# Build output
/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Rust
Cargo.lock.bak

# Local runtime data
/data
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# A key file generated by a development run, wherever the process was started.
# It is local runtime state, and a generated key must never be committed.
**/data/
*.key

# Configuration and secrets
.env
.env.local
darkhold.toml
!darkhold.toml.example

# Frontend
frontend/node_modules/
node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/.vite/
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Compiled Python from scripts/. Committed once by accident; the churn on every
# interpreter version change is not worth a diff entry.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The global gitignore has `*credentials*`, to keep real secrets out of git.
# That rule silently swallows `migrations/*/0096_source_credentials.sql` — a
# schema migration whose name describes the TABLE it creates, and which holds no
# secret at all. It stayed untracked while the migration applied locally, so the
# vault worked here and would have been missing on every other checkout, with
# `git add -A` reporting success the whole time. Repo's own rules win over the
# global ones, so this negation restores it.
!migrations/**/*credentials*.sql

# Same trap, second instance: crates/app/tests/m53_source_credentials.rs. It
# describes the TABLE it tests and holds no secret, and the global rule's
# `*credentials*` swallowed it anyway - so it sat untracked from 2026-10-04
# until the rename, when it failed to compile because nothing had renamed its
# crate references. The file is 36 KB of tests that only ever existed on this
# machine: every other checkout was missing them, and `git add -A` reported
# success the whole time. Same rule as above - an explicit negation per file,
# never a loosened global rule.
!crates/**/*credentials*.rs

# Same trap for any future file whose NAME is about credentials rather than
# contents. Prefer an explicit negation per file over loosening the global rule,
# which exists to do its job for actual secrets.

